(?!Telomeres are repetitive nucleotide sequences (TTAGGG in humans);ref.1!?) capping chromosome ends, preventing DNA degradation and fusion. With each cell division, telomeres shorten due to the (?!end-replication problem;ref.1!?). When critically short:

\begin{itemize}
\item (?!DNA damage responses trigger cellular senescence;ref.2!?)
\item Accumulated senescent cells drive tissue aging
\item Organ systems fail progressively
\item Lifespan approaches maximum $\sim$120 years
\end{itemize}

(?!Short telomeres implicate diseases: cancer, neurodegeneration, cardiovascular disorders, immune dysfunction;refs.2,9!?) Paradoxically, (?!excessively long telomeres increase cancer risk;ref.10!?) by allowing mutated cells to proliferate indefinitely.

